    "https://adb.zuv.uni-heidelberg.de/info/INFO_FDB$.startup?MODUL=LS&SESSID=0&M1=1&M2=0&M3=0&PRO=40709": {
      "title": "PhD Position in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d)",
      "description": "Stellenausschreibungen an der Universität Heidelberg Startseite Universität Fakultäten Einrichtungen Studium Forschung Internationales Exzellenzstrategie Alumni Beschäftigte Doktoranden Freunde & Förderer Presse & News Wirtschaft Schnelleinstieg Fokus Flüchtlinge Nobelpreisträger Daten und Fakten Rankings heiEDUCATION Qualitätsentwicklung Gute wissenschaftliche Praxis Mensa-Speiseplan Stellen und Karriere Sie sind hier: Universität Heidelberg > Universitätsverwaltung > Stellenangebote PhD Position in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d) Heidelberg University is a comprehensive university with a strong focus on research and international standards. With around 32,200 students and 9,540 employees, including numerous top researchers, it is a globally respected institution that also has outstanding economic significance for the Rhine-Neckar metropolitan region. The Biomedical Microfluidics Group at Heidelberg University develops ultrasound-driven devices to isolate extracellular vesicles from microlitre samples. We study how acoustic fields trap and separate these particles so that they can be analysed for signs of disease, including cancer. We invite applications for the following part-time position (75%) for up to three years. The earliest start date is 1 February 2027. A later start is possible by agreement. PhD Position in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d) Your tasks: Build and test prototype microfluidic devices for vesicle enrichment. Measure how acoustic frequency, power, channel geometry and material choice affect particle trapping and separation. Combine ultrasound transducers, electronics and microfluidic components in a working measurement system. Write control routines and test how reproducibly the device performs. Work with biologists, chemists and clinical researchers to evaluate the devices and connect separation experiments with downstream analysis Publish your findings and present them at international conferences Your profile: A good master’s degree in electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, physics, chemistry, biomedical engineering or a related field. We particularly welcome electrical engineers. Hands-on experience from coursework, a thesis or research projects in electronics, instrumentation, acoustics, microfluidics, fabrication or experimental physics Curiosity about the physics behind your measurements, initiative and an interest in learning new methods. Programming or data analysis in Python or MATLAB is an advantage, as is experience with finite-element modelling, transducers, microfabrication or rapid prototyping Good communication skills in English and an interest in interdisciplinary teamwork. Applicants can join without prior experience in extracellular vesicles or biological assays. We offer: Close doctoral supervision, training in the experimental and analytical methods your project requires, and room to pursue your own research ideas Access to microfabrication, acoustofluidics and bioanalysis facilities and collaboration with colleagues in engineering, the natural sciences and medicine Support for professional development and participation in international conferences Remuneration is based on pay grade 13 (75%) under the TV-L collective agreement. The monthly gross salary for the respective pay grade is determined in accordance with the collective agreement (TV-L Remuneration Schedule) and the applicable assignment to a salary step. The current salary tables for the State of Baden-Württemberg can be found here: lbv.landbw.de/leistungen-und-services/gehaltstabellen . Please email your application to michael.gerlt@imseam.uni-heidelberg.de by 03 December 2026 . Merge your CV, a short letter outlining your motivation and relevant experience, academic transcripts and contact details for two referees into one PDF. You may include a brief example of a relevant technical or research project in the same file. Use the subject line “PhD Application – [Last Name]”, replacing the placeholder with your surname. Only applications using this subject line and a single merged PDF will be reviewed. Informal enquiries are welcome. Please direct enquiries to michael.gerlt@bme.lth.se . Heidelberg University stands for equal opportunities and diversity. Persons with severe disabilities will be given preference if they are equally qualified. Information on job advertisements and the collection of personal data is available at www.uni-heidelberg.de/en/job-market . Informationen für Bewerberinnen und Bewerber Datenschutz Beschäftigte in Wissenschaft, Verwaltung und Technik Berufungsverfahren Ausbildung Informationen für Universitätsmitglieder Diese Seite als RSS-Feed abonnieren Weitere Informationen: Startseite der Universität | Kontakt (Universität) | Impressum | Universitätsverwaltung | Suche (Universität) | Suche (UV) | Datenschutz Diese Seite wurde automatisch aus der Oracle 11g Datenbank erzeugt"
    },
    "https://adb.zuv.uni-heidelberg.de/info/INFO_FDB$.startup?MODUL=LS&SESSID=0&M1=1&M2=0&M3=0&PRO=40711": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Acoustofluidic Loading of Lipid Vesicles (f/m/d)",
      "description": "Stellenausschreibungen an der Universität Heidelberg Startseite Universität Fakultäten Einrichtungen Studium Forschung Internationales Exzellenzstrategie Alumni Beschäftigte Doktoranden Freunde & Förderer Presse & News Wirtschaft Schnelleinstieg Fokus Flüchtlinge Nobelpreisträger Daten und Fakten Rankings heiEDUCATION Qualitätsentwicklung Gute wissenschaftliche Praxis Mensa-Speiseplan Stellen und Karriere Sie sind hier: Universität Heidelberg > Universitätsverwaltung > Stellenangebote Postdoctoral Researcher in Acoustofluidic Loading of Lipid Vesicles (f/m/d) Heidelberg University is a comprehensive university with a strong focus on research and international standards. With around 32,200 students and 9,540 employees, including numerous top researchers, it is a globally respected institution that also has outstanding economic significance for the Rhine-Neckar metropolitan region. The AcouLiVe project brings together the Biomedical Microfluidics Group at Heidelberg University and the Biomembrane Engineering Group at the Max Planck Institute for Medical Research. We use ultrasound to load therapeutic cargo into lipid vesicles in continuous flow while preserving cargo activity and membrane integrity. Heidelberg University is recruiting for the following full-time position (100%), starting in 2027 on a date to be agreed. The initial appointment is for 12 months, with the possibility of a further 12-month extension. Postdoctoral Researcher in Acoustofluidic Loading of Lipid Vesicles (f/m/d) Your tasks: Engineer lipid vesicles with controlled composition, fluidity, stiffness, asymmetry and surface chemistry. Develop a continuous-flow µSonicator and determine how frequency, power, duty cycle, residence time, flow rate and device geometry affect membrane permeabilization. Load mRNA, proteins and ribonucleoprotein complexes into vesicles. Measure loading efficiency, leakage, recovery, stability and membrane integrity using particle sizing, microscopy and biochemical assays. Relate membrane properties and acoustic exposure to cellular uptake, cargo release and activity. Assess GFP expression, genome editing and cytotoxicity. Publish and present results, help supervise students in both laboratories and contribute to grant proposals. Your profile: A PhD in biomedical or chemical engineering, pharmaceutical sciences, biophysics, physics, chemistry, biochemistry, nanomedicine or a related discipline. Strong experimental skills in either lipid-vesicle engineering (e.g. formulation, encapsulation or intracellular delivery) or microfluidic engineering (e.g. acoustofluidics, transducers or device fabrication), and an interest in learning the complementary methods. Experience in quantitative experimental design and data analysis, the ability to work independently and collaboratively, and excellent written and spoken English. Experience with DLS, NTA, fluorescence assays, mammalian cell culture, confocal microscopy, flow cytometry, rapid prototyping or data analysis in Python or MATLAB would be an advantage. We offer : Joint supervision by Michael Gerlt and Kevin Jahnke, with access to both laboratories’ facilities for microfabrication, acoustofluidics, vesicle preparation and characterization, microscopy, flow cytometry and cell culture. Mentoring and opportunities to develop an independent research profile through student supervision, international conferences and follow-up proposals to the DFG and European funding programmes. Remuneration is based on pay grade 13 under the TV-L collective agreement. The monthly gross salary for the respective pay grade is determined in accordance with the collective agreement (TV-L Remuneration Schedule) and the applicable assignment to a salary step. The current salary tables for the State of Baden-Württemberg can be found here: lbv.landbw.de/leistungen-und-services/gehaltstabellen . The position is generally suitable for job sharing. Please apply by 03 December 2026 by emailing a single PDF to both michael.gerlt@imseam.uni-heidelberg.de and kevin.jahnke@mr.mpg.de . Use the subject line “Postdoc Application AcouLiVe [Last Name]”. Include your CV, a cover letter describing your motivation and relevant experience, contact details for two academic references, a project idea explaining your contribution to AcouLiVe (up to one page) and a portfolio of relevant experimental and technical skills (up to half a page). Heidelberg University stands for equal opportunities and diversity. Persons with severe disabilities will be given preference if they are equally qualified. Information on job advertisements and the collection of personal data is available at www.uni-heidelberg.de/en/job-market . Informationen für Bewerberinnen und Bewerber Datenschutz Beschäftigte in Wissenschaft, Verwaltung und Technik Berufungsverfahren Ausbildung Informationen für Universitätsmitglieder Diese Seite als RSS-Feed abonnieren Weitere Informationen: Startseite der Universität | Kontakt (Universität) | Impressum | Universitätsverwaltung | Suche (Universität) | Suche (UV) | Datenschutz Diese Seite wurde automatisch aus der Oracle 11g Datenbank erzeugt"
    },
    "https://adb.zuv.uni-heidelberg.de/info/INFO_FDB$.startup?MODUL=LS&SESSID=0&M1=1&M2=0&M3=0&PRO=40710": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d)",
      "description": "Stellenausschreibungen an der Universität Heidelberg Startseite Universität Fakultäten Einrichtungen Studium Forschung Internationales Exzellenzstrategie Alumni Beschäftigte Doktoranden Freunde & Förderer Presse & News Wirtschaft Schnelleinstieg Fokus Flüchtlinge Nobelpreisträger Daten und Fakten Rankings heiEDUCATION Qualitätsentwicklung Gute wissenschaftliche Praxis Mensa-Speiseplan Stellen und Karriere Sie sind hier: Universität Heidelberg > Universitätsverwaltung > Stellenangebote Postdoc in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d) Heidelberg University is a comprehensive university with a strong focus on research and international standards. With around 32,200 students and 9,540 employees, including numerous top researchers, it is a globally respected institution that also has outstanding economic significance for the Rhine-Neckar metropolitan region. The Biomedical Microfluidics Group at Heidelberg University develops ultrasound-driven devices to isolate extracellular vesicles from microlitre samples. We study how acoustic fields trap and separate these particles so that they can be analysed for signs of disease, including cancer. We invite applications for the following full-time position (100%) for up to two years. The earliest start date is 1 February 2027. A later start is possible by agreement. Postdoc in Acoustofluidics and Biomedical Device Engineering (f/m/d) Your tasks: Lead the design and experimental evaluation of devices for vesicle enrichment and separation. Determine how acoustic actuation, geometry and materials affect particle manipulation. Combine ultrasound transducers, electronics and microfluidic components in a working measurement system. Automate measurements and controls to quantify performance across experiments. Work with biologists, chemists and clinical researchers to evaluate the devices and connect separation experiments with downstream analysis Lead publications, present results at international conferences and help supervise doctoral and master’s students Your profile: A PhD in electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, physics, chemistry, biomedical engineering or a related field. We particularly welcome electrical engineers. Experience building and characterising experimental systems, with expertise in acoustics, transducers, instrumentation, microfluidics, microsystems or device fabrication The ability to design experiments independently and interpret quantitative data critically. Programming and data analysis in Python or MATLAB are highly desirable. Automation, finite-element modelling or microfabrication experience is an advantage. Excellent communication skills in English and an interest in interdisciplinary teamwork. You enjoy supporting junior researchers. Applicants can join without prior experience in extracellular vesicles or biological assays. We offer: Scientific independence to pursue your own ideas within the project and opportunities to gain experience in project leadership and student supervision Access to microfabrication, acoustofluidics and bioanalysis facilities and collaboration with colleagues in engineering, the natural sciences and medicine Support for professional development and participation in international conferences. You can also build your international research network. Remuneration is based on pay grade 13 (100%) under the TV-L collective agreement. The monthly gross salary for the respective pay grade is determined in accordance with the collective agreement (TV-L Remuneration Schedule) and the applicable assignment to a salary step. The current salary tables for the State of Baden-Württemberg can be found here: lbv.landbw.de/leistungen-und-services/gehaltstabellen . The position is generally suitable for job sharing. Please email your application to michael.gerlt@imseam.uni-heidelberg.de by 03 December 2026 . Merge your CV (including your publication list), a short letter outlining your motivation and relevant experience, academic transcripts and contact details for two referees into one PDF. You may include a brief example of a relevant technical or research project in the same file. Use the subject line “Postdoc Application – [Last Name]”, replacing the placeholder with your surname. Only applications using this subject line and a single merged PDF will be reviewed. Informal enquiries are welcome. Heidelberg University stands for equal opportunities and diversity. Persons with severe disabilities will be given preference if they are equally qualified. Information on job advertisements and the collection of personal data is available at www.uni-heidelberg.de/en/job-market . Informationen für Bewerberinnen und Bewerber Datenschutz Beschäftigte in Wissenschaft, Verwaltung und Technik Berufungsverfahren Ausbildung Informationen für Universitätsmitglieder Diese Seite als RSS-Feed abonnieren Weitere Informationen: Startseite der Universität | Kontakt (Universität) | Impressum | Universitätsverwaltung | Suche (Universität) | Suche (UV) | Datenschutz Diese Seite wurde automatisch aus der Oracle 11g Datenbank erzeugt"
    }
